Participant Information Sheet and Consent Form
Study: A Phase 1, Randomised, Dose-Escalation Study of the Safety and Tolerability of StudyDrugX in
Healthy Adult Volunteers
Protocol: SDX-101 (synthetic) Document version: v2.0, dated 11 May 2026
Site: Northside Research Clinic, 45 Sydney Road, Brunswick VIC 3056
Principal Investigator: Dr Sarah Mitchell (MBBS, FRACP)
Participant code: S004
1. What is this study about?
This study tests how safe StudyDrugX is and how well it is tolerated when healthy adults take it at two
dose levels, 100 mg and 200 mg. It is the first time the drug is being given to people.
2. What will happen if I take part?
You will attend four visits about 14 days apart: a screening visit, two dosing visits where you take
StudyDrugX at the clinic, and a follow-up visit. At each visit we will check your heart rate and blood
pressure and take a blood sample for haemoglobin, white blood cell count, ALT (a liver enzyme) and
creatinine (a kidney marker).
3. What are the possible risks?
Side effects reported with similar medicines include nausea, headache and mild skin rash. Medicines such
as paracetamol, ondansetron or loratadine may be given at the clinic to treat these.
4. What has changed in this version (v2.0)?
Since the study began, some participants had a temporary rise in ALT, a liver enzyme, after their second
dose. The protocol was amended on 11 May 2026: liver blood tests are now reviewed by the investigator
before each dose, and an ALT result above the upper limit of normal (40 U/L) is repeated at the next visit.
Participants who were already in the study were asked to sign this version.
5. How is my information protected?
Study records identify you by your participant code (S004). Your name appears only on this consent form,
which is stored securely at the site. Staff who review study reports see the code, not your name.
6. Do I have to take part?
No. Taking part is voluntary and you may withdraw at any time without giving a reason. Withdrawing will
not affect your usual medical care.

